# services/scrum-poker-core

The isomorphic heart of Scrum Poker (scrum-poker.shatteredarchive.dev), imported unchanged by
both `apps/scrum-poker-server` and `apps/scrum-poker-client` — the same arrangement
`services/kingdom-tactics-engine` has with the KT client/server pair, and enforced the same way:
`tsconfig.json` sets `"types": []`, so a shipped module that reaches for `fs`, `process` or a DOM
global fails the build.

Four modules, all pure:

- `types.ts` — the domain. The important distinction is `Room` (SERVER-side record: every raw
  vote plus the room's `hostToken`) versus `RoomView` (the ONLY shape that goes over the wire).
  `Participant` splits identity in two for the same reason: `id` is PUBLIC (broadcast in every
  roster, so it is a render key and nothing more) while `secret` is the private re-attach
  credential. They were once one field, and that let any member rejoin as any other — casting
  votes in their name and reading their hidden estimate, since a viewer always sees their own
  card. Anything that adds a field here must decide which side of that line it is on.
- `deck.ts` — estimate-card decks. `parseDeck`/`formatDeck` handle the comma-separated string the
  settings dialog edits; `validateDeck` is the single rule set both the dialog and the server use.
  `cardValue` deliberately returns null for `?`/`☕` so they never skew an average.
- `room.ts` — every state transition as `(room, …, now) -> room`. Nothing reads a clock or mints
  an id; the caller supplies both, which is what makes an hour of idle time a one-line test.
  `joinRoom` re-attaches on the replayed SECRET and returns the row the server settled on;
  it never matches on the public id.
  `toRoomView` is the single chokepoint that enforces vote hiding — while a room is unrevealed and
  `hideUntilRevealed` is on, other participants' votes are erased from the payload server-side
  (the viewer still sees their own), so hiding is not a client-side rendering choice.
- `protocol.ts` — the `/ws/scrum` contract plus `parseScrumClientMessage`, which treats its input
  as hostile: it validates shape and primitive types for every field, discards unknown settings
  keys, and returns `undefined` instead of throwing. Value RANGES are not checked here —
  `applySettingsPatch` owns those, so there is exactly one definition of a legal deck.
  `applySettingsPatch` checks TYPES as well as values, which looks redundant beside the parser
  but is not: it is also the validator for records read off DISK, where nothing has vouched for
  the shape, and throwing there used to stop scrum-poker-server booting entirely.
